  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Periods of haze and smoke persist across the region into\n   tonight. Low rain shower and storm chances.\n\n-  Monsoon moisture surge returns Tuesday through Thursday.\n   Localized flash flooding and a few severe thunderstorms\n   possible.\n\n-  Hot temperatures are expected this week, with highs reaching\n   the 90s up to 106 degrees in the lower elevations of Idaho.\n\nThis afternoon through Wednesday...Upper level monsoonal moisture\nwill continue to stream across the Northern Rockies into Tuesday \nmorning, bringing persistent high and mid-level cloud cover and \nisolated light showers. Smoke and haze from wildfires in Oregon \nand Washington will intermittently degrade air quality, though a \ndeveloping easterly pressure gradient tonight may offer some \nlocalized clearing.\n\nAtmospheric moisture deepens significantly on Tuesday, with \nprecipitable water (PWAT) values climbing to 200-250% of normal \nfor the region. However, abundant morning cloud cover will likely \nlimit surface instability and overall thunderstorm potential. By \nTuesday afternoon, high-resolution guidance indicates scattered \nthunderstorm development from Lemhi County into Southwest Montana,\nwhere brief heavy rainfall and erratic wind gusts exceeding 40 \nmph will be the primary threats.\n\nBy Wednesday afternoon, the threat shifts slightly northward. \nAreas south of U.S. Highway 12 will see an increased risk of \nlocally heavy rainfall capable of triggering debris flows or flash\nflooding. The overall magnitude of this threat remains highly \nconditional on morning clearing and the resulting realized \ninstability.\n\nIt will also be quite warm to hot. NBM temperatures are on the \nwarmer side compared to most guidance and would bring \ntemperatures near heat advisory levels to the lower elevations of\nnorth-central Idaho. However, there is considerable uncertainty \nregarding how much clouds and smoke may impact the high \ntemperature forecast. Still advisories may be needed for the \nWednesday/Thursday time period if confidence increases. \n\nThursday through the Weekend...Thursday presents a more dynamic \nsetup as the region transitions from deep monsoonal moisture to a \ndrier, more sheared southwesterly flow. This combination of \nenhanced vertical wind shear and increased instability could \nsupport strong to severe thunderstorms, making Thursday a key \nperiod to monitor.\n\nBy Friday and into the weekend, the synoptic pattern shifts \ntoward near-critical fire weather conditions. Increasing westerly \nwinds, a drying air mass, and a sharp decline in shower activity \nwill combine to elevate fire danger across the region.",
